dns: use the noop OS configurator on Android

In Android, system DNS is owned by netd's DnsResolver and is only
configurable over Binder: there is no /etc/resolv.conf, so upstream's
Linux probe lands on the "direct" manager, whose GetBaseConfig
failure on the missing file aborts dns.Manager.Set before the
quad-100 resolver is configured, killing MagicDNS. Do what the
GOOS=android build does and use the noop manager: quad-100 still
serves MagicDNS and forwards to the tailnet's resolvers.

diff --git a/net/dns/manager_linux.go b/net/dns/manager_linux.go
index 392b64b..f3ce605 100644
--- a/net/dns/manager_linux.go
+++ b/net/dns/manager_linux.go
@@ -69,6 +69,17 @@ func NewOSConfigurator(logf logger.Logf, health *health.Tracker, bus *eventbus.B
 		return NewNoopManager()
 	}
 
+	// In Android, system DNS is owned by netd's DnsResolver and is only
+	// configurable over Binder; there is no /etc/resolv.conf, so the probe
+	// below would land on the "direct" manager, whose GetBaseConfig
+	// failure on the missing file aborts dns.Manager.Set before the
+	// quad-100 resolver is configured, killing MagicDNS. Use the noop
+	// manager like the GOOS=android build does: quad-100 still serves
+	// MagicDNS and forwards to the tailnet's resolvers.
+	if _, err := os.Stat("/data/adb/tailscale"); err == nil {
+		return NewNoopManager()
+	}
+
 	env := newOSConfigEnv{
 		fs:              directFS{},
 		resolvconfStyle: resolvconfStyle,
